From e6459dd9b59a890f65b966497732ff0f79a72279 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dhruv Rakeshbhai Patel <dpatel5469@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 06:11:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] r8169: keep single-packet traffic at DIM level 0

With adaptive RX moderation on, request-response traffic lost about 6%
in transactions per second. DIM leaves level 0 whenever its statistics
change by more than 10% between two windows of 64 events, which happens
all the time at several thousand transactions per second. At level 1
there is nothing to gain, since every reply is a single packet that
waits for the timer, so DIM goes back, but the excursions still cost
about one transaction in six the full 40 microseconds.

Do not show DIM a poll that received a single packet while the level is
0. Such a poll means that the CPU kept up with the NIC. Request-response
traffic is made of such polls, so it never leaves level 0, while bulk
traffic, which batches two or more packets per poll even at level 0,
still does.

Above level 0 every poll is shown to DIM, so that it can always step
back down, for example when bulk traffic is followed by
request-response traffic at a high level.

Co-authored-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 drivers/net/ethernet/realtek/r8169_main.c | 16 ++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/realtek/r8169_main.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/realtek/r8169_main.c
index ececcfc14..b32193bc5 100644
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/realtek/r8169_main.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/realtek/r8169_main.c
@@ -5103,10 +5103,22 @@ static void rtl_task(struct work_struct *work)
 	}
 }
 
-static void rtl_rx_dim_update(struct rtl8169_private *tp)
+/* @pkts is the number of packets the poll received. At level 0 a poll with a
+ * single packet means that the CPU kept up with the NIC, so a longer timer
+ * would only add latency, and request-response traffic looks exactly like
+ * that. Such a poll is not shown to DIM, which then never leaves level 0 for
+ * it. Above level 0 every poll counts, so that DIM can always step back down,
+ * for example when bulk traffic is followed by request-response traffic.
+ * profile_ix is only changed by net_dim() here in NAPI context, and by the
+ * reset while the ethtool flag is off.
+ */
+static void rtl_rx_dim_update(struct rtl8169_private *tp, int pkts)
 {
 	struct dim_sample sample = {};
 
+	if (pkts < 2 && !tp->rx_dim.profile_ix)
+		return;
+
 	dim_update_sample(++tp->rx_dim_events, tp->rx_dim_packets,
 			  tp->rx_dim_bytes, &sample);
 	net_dim(&tp->rx_dim, &sample);
@@ -5127,7 +5139,7 @@ static int rtl8169_poll(struct napi_struct *napi, int budget)
 	 * rate where the poll may never complete.
 	 */
 	if (work_done && READ_ONCE(tp->rx_dim_enabled))
-		rtl_rx_dim_update(tp);
+		rtl_rx_dim_update(tp, work_done);
 
 	if (work_done < budget && napi_complete_done(napi, work_done))
 		rtl_irq_enable(tp);
-- 
2.43.0

